codex
The constructor-split fixed-semantics redesign confirms the language gap: after one circularity step, the Haskell backend retains a symbolic `V:Val`; even `isStr(V) = true` does not refine it to `str(CS)`, so the next fixed `set(str)` call is unreachable to the prover. This is exactly the backend limitation covered by the Kit’s guarded-projection idiom. I’m restoring that sanctioned construction, but keeping the audit conclusion precise: the twins are derived sort-dispatch lemmas (not new program-result oracles), their guards equal the fixed static match domain, and the static fixed-semantic equations are machine-checked separately.
apply patch
